IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), 1992 c. 4.
-- PART XIIA, INCAPACITY FOR WORK, ss 171A to 171G (src:4252-4330).
-- LAYER 1: the law, objectively.  The source is a plain-text rendering made by a converter
-- script; it is not the official text and nothing here is "verified against the official text".
--
-- How to read a result (the rule of 2026-10-29):
--   JUST FALSE / JUST TRUE : the text says no / yes in its own words (each "no" branch quotes them).
--   NOTHING                : not covered (each branch says why: silent, list not closed, includes,
--                            undefined term, points outside the text, limited by its own wording).
--   A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Money is a NUMBER in pounds sterling.  Rates, periods and lengths that regulations supply are
-- INPUTS.  Dates are `Date day month year`.
-- "Week" in this Part is any period of 7 days (s 171G(2)), so 8 weeks are 56 days and 21 weeks are 147 days.

§ `Part XIIA -- Incapacity for work`

§§ `s 171A Test of incapacity for work`

-- (1) "For the purposes of this Act, save as otherwise expressly provided, whether a person is capable or incapable of work shall
-- be determined in accordance with the provisions of this Part of this Act."
-- src:4254 | (1) For the purposes of this Act, save as otherwise expressly provided, whether a person is capable or incapable of work shall be determined in accordance with the provisions of this Part of this Act.
@ref s 171A(1) (src:4254)
GIVEN `another provision of this Act expressly provides otherwise for the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171A(1) this Part determines whether the person is capable or incapable of work` MEANS
    IF `another provision of this Act expressly provides otherwise for the purpose`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "save as otherwise expressly provided"; the provision that so provides answers
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- (2) "Regulations may make provision as to (a) the information or evidence required ..., and (b) the manner ..., and may provide
-- that if a person without good cause fails to provide that information or evidence, or to do so in the manner required, he shall
-- be treated as capable of work."  No such regulations: the text alone cannot answer.  With them, a failed "if" is counted as the
-- text's no (reading (a), for a lawyer).
-- src:4255 | (2) Regulations may make provision as to—
-- src:4256 | (a) the information or evidence required for the purpose of determining whether a person is capable or incapable of work, and
-- src:4257 | (b) the manner in which that information or evidence is to be provided,
-- src:4258 | and may provide that if a person without good cause fails to provide that information or evidence, or to do so in the manner required, he shall be treated as capable of work.
@ref s 171A(2) (src:4255-4258)
GIVEN `regulations under subsection (2) provide that a failure without good cause leads to treatment as capable of work` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person fails to provide the information or evidence, or to do so in the manner required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has good cause for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171A(2) the person is treated as capable of work` MEANS
    IF NOT `regulations under subsection (2) provide that a failure without good cause leads to treatment as capable of work`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may ... provide that" the person is treated as capable of work
    ELSE IF `the person fails to provide the information or evidence, or to do so in the manner required` AND (NOT `the person has good cause for the failure`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if a person without good cause fails to provide that information or evidence, or to do so in the manner required, he shall be treated as capable of work" (a failed "if"; reading (a))

-- (2A) "In subsection (2)(a) above the reference to such information or evidence as is there mentioned includes information or
-- evidence capable of being used for assisting or encouraging the person in question to obtain work or enhance his prospects of obtaining it."
-- src:4259 | (2A) In subsection (2)(a) above the reference to such information or evidence as is there mentioned includes information or evidence capable of being used for assisting or encouraging the person in question to obtain work or enhance his prospects of obtaining it.
@ref s 171A(2A) (src:4259)
GIVEN `the information or evidence is capable of being used for assisting or encouraging the person to obtain work or enhance the person's prospects of obtaining it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171A(2A) the information or evidence is within subsection (2)(a)` MEANS
    IF `the information or evidence is capable of being used for assisting or encouraging the person to obtain work or enhance the person's prospects of obtaining it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "includes" adds a case and does not close the list of what is required

-- (3) "Regulations may provide that in any case where it falls to be determined whether a person is capable of work (a) he may be
-- called to attend for such medical examination as may be required in accordance with regulations, and (b) if he fails without good
-- cause to attend for or submit himself to such examination, he shall be treated as capable of work."
-- src:4260 | (3) Regulations may provide that in any case where it falls to be determined whether a person is capable of work—
-- src:4261 | (a) he may be called to attend for such medical examination as may be required in accordance with regulations, and
-- src:4262 | (b) if he fails without good cause to attend for or submit himself to such examination, he shall be treated as capable of work.
@ref s 171A(3) (src:4260-4262)
GIVEN `regulations under subsection (3) so provide` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person was called to attend for a medical examination in accordance with regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person fails to attend for or submit to the examination` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has good cause for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171A(3) the person is treated as capable of work` MEANS
    IF NOT `regulations under subsection (3) so provide`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may provide that" the person is treated as capable of work
    ELSE IF     `the person was called to attend for a medical examination in accordance with regulations`
            AND `the person fails to attend for or submit to the examination`
            AND (NOT `the person has good cause for the failure`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if he fails without good cause to attend for or submit himself to such examination, he shall be treated as capable of work" (a failed "if"; reading (a))

-- (4) Regulations may prescribe the matters that bear on good cause: a power (inert, machinery; see NOTES).  The good cause
-- itself is a plain input to (2) and (3).

-- (5) "All information supplied in pursuance of this section shall be taken for all purposes to be information relating to social security."
-- src:4266 | (5) All information supplied in pursuance of this section shall be taken for all purposes to be information relating to social security.
@ref s 171A(5) (src:4266)
GIVEN `the information was supplied in pursuance of section 171A` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171A(5) the information is information relating to social security` MEANS
    IF `the information was supplied in pursuance of section 171A`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks of "information supplied in pursuance of this section"

§§ `s 171B The own occupation test`

-- (1) "Where a person has been engaged in remunerative work for more than 8 weeks in the 21 weeks immediately preceding the day with
-- respect to which it falls to be determined whether he is or was incapable of work, the own occupation test is applicable in his case."
-- "Remunerative work" is defined by regulations (subsection (6)), so the number of weeks is a plain input.  The weeks are totalled
-- (fork in NOTES).  Councillor work is not counted (s 171F(1)): the caller leaves it out of the count.
-- src:4268 | (1) Where a person has been engaged in remunerative work for more than 8 weeks in the 21 weeks immediately preceding the day with respect to which it falls to be determined whether he is or was incapable of work, the own occupation test is applicable in his case.
@ref s 171B(1) (src:4268)
GIVEN `the number of the 21 weeks immediately preceding the day in which the person was engaged in remunerative work` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171B(1) the own occupation test is applicable` MEANS
    IF `the number of the 21 weeks immediately preceding the day in which the person was engaged in remunerative work` GREATER THAN 8
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection says when the test "is applicable" ("more than 8 weeks") and is not an "only where": s 171B(3) makes the test "remain applicable" for a person with 8 weeks or fewer who has a carried-forward determination, so a JUST FALSE here would contradict it; s 171C(1) takes up the case

-- (2) "The own occupation test is whether he is incapable by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement of doing
-- work which he could reasonably be expected to do in the course of the occupation in which he was so engaged."
-- src:4269 | (2) The own occupation test is whether he is incapable by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement of doing work which he could reasonably be expected to do in the course of the occupation in which he was so engaged.
@ref s 171B(2) (src:4269)
GIVEN `the person is incapable by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement of doing work which the person could reasonably be expected to do in the course of the occupation in which the person was so engaged` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171B(2) the person is incapable of work on the own occupation test` MEANS
    IF `the person is incapable by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement of doing work which the person could reasonably be expected to do in the course of the occupation in which the person was so engaged`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The own occupation test is whether he is incapable by reason of some specific disease or bodily or mental disablement of doing work which he could reasonably be expected to do in the course of the occupation in which he was so engaged"

-- (3) The nouns: the state of the test on a given day.
DECLARE `s 171B(3) status of the own occupation test` IS ONE OF
    `s 171B(3) the test remains applicable`
    `s 171B(3) the stated period has ended`
    `s 171B(3) the text does not say whether the test applies`
    `s 171B(3) there is no determination to carry forward`

-- (3), the last paragraph: "a 'spell of incapacity' means a series of 4 or more consecutive days of incapacity for work".
-- The series: the days of incapacity (as serial numbers, sorted and without repeats) cut into maximal runs of consecutive days.
-- src:4274 | For this purpose a “spell of incapacity” means a series of 4 or more consecutive days of incapacity for work; and any two such spells not separated by a period of more than 8 weeks shall be treated as one spell of incapacity.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4274)
GIVEN start IS A NUMBER
      end IS A NUMBER
      days IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF Period
`s 171B(3) the series of consecutive days from` MEANS
    CONSIDER days
    WHEN EMPTY THEN LIST (Period WITH `the first day` IS Date start, `the last day` IS Date end)
    WHEN d FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        IF d EQUALS end PLUS 1
        THEN `s 171B(3) the series of consecutive days from` start d rest
        ELSE (Period WITH `the first day` IS Date start, `the last day` IS Date end) FOLLOWED BY (`s 171B(3) the series of consecutive days from` d d rest)

-- src:4274 | For this purpose a “spell of incapacity” means a series of 4 or more consecutive days of incapacity for work; and any two such spells not separated by a period of more than 8 weeks shall be treated as one spell of incapacity.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4274)
GIVEN `the days of incapacity for work` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A LIST OF Period
`s 171B(3) the series of consecutive days of incapacity for work` MEANS
    CONSIDER sortedDays
    WHEN EMPTY THEN EMPTY
    WHEN d FOLLOWED BY rest THEN `s 171B(3) the series of consecutive days from` d d rest
    WHERE
        sortedDays MEANS sort (nub (map (GIVEN x YIELD Day x) `the days of incapacity for work`))

-- (3), the last paragraph: "any two such spells not separated by a period of more than 8 weeks shall be treated as one spell
-- of incapacity."  The days between two spells are the separation; 8 weeks are 56 days (s 171G(2)); a separation of exactly 56 days
-- is not "more than 8 weeks", so the spells merge.  Merging chains from left to right.
-- src:4274 | For this purpose a “spell of incapacity” means a series of 4 or more consecutive days of incapacity for work; and any two such spells not separated by a period of more than 8 weeks shall be treated as one spell of incapacity.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4274)
GIVEN current IS A Period
      later IS A LIST OF Period
GIVETH A LIST OF Period
`s 171B(3) the spells merged from` MEANS
    CONSIDER later
    WHEN EMPTY THEN LIST current
    WHEN next FOLLOWED BY more THEN
        IF (Day (next's `the first day`)) MINUS (Day (current's `the last day`)) MINUS 1 AT MOST 56
        THEN `s 171B(3) the spells merged from` (Period WITH `the first day` IS current's `the first day`, `the last day` IS next's `the last day`) more
        ELSE current FOLLOWED BY (`s 171B(3) the spells merged from` next more)

-- src:4274 | For this purpose a “spell of incapacity” means a series of 4 or more consecutive days of incapacity for work; and any two such spells not separated by a period of more than 8 weeks shall be treated as one spell of incapacity.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4274)
GIVEN `the days of incapacity for work` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A LIST OF Period
`s 171B(3) the spells of incapacity` MEANS
    CONSIDER qualifying
    WHEN EMPTY THEN EMPTY
    WHEN firstSpell FOLLOWED BY more THEN `s 171B(3) the spells merged from` firstSpell more
    WHERE
        series MEANS `s 171B(3) the series of consecutive days of incapacity for work` `the days of incapacity for work`
        qualifying MEANS filter (GIVEN p YIELD (((Day (p's `the last day`)) MINUS (Day (p's `the first day`))) PLUS 1) AT LEAST 4) series

-- (3) "the spell of incapacity beginning with that day or, as the case may be, in which that day falls".
-- src:4273 | that test remains applicable in his case until the end of the spell of incapacity beginning with that day or, as the case may be, in which that day falls, or until the 197th day of incapacity for work in that spell, whichever is the earlier.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4273)
GIVEN `the days of incapacity for work` IS A LIST OF DATE
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 171B(3) the spell in which the day falls` MEANS
    CONSIDER containing
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING
    WHEN found FOLLOWED BY more THEN JUST found
    WHERE
        spells MEANS `s 171B(3) the spells of incapacity` `the days of incapacity for work`
        containing MEANS filter (GIVEN p YIELD ((Day (p's `the first day`)) AT MOST (Day day)) AND ((Day day) AT MOST (Day (p's `the last day`)))) spells

-- (3) the nth day of incapacity for work (serial numbers, sorted).
-- src:4273 | that test remains applicable in his case until the end of the spell of incapacity beginning with that day or, as the case may be, in which that day falls, or until the 197th day of incapacity for work in that spell, whichever is the earlier.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4273)
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      days IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 171B(3) the nth day of incapacity for work` MEANS
    CONSIDER days
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING
    WHEN d FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        IF n EQUALS 1
        THEN JUST d
        ELSE `s 171B(3) the nth day of incapacity for work` (n MINUS 1) rest

-- (3) "or until the 197th day of incapacity for work in that spell": the days of incapacity that lie in the spell, counted from its start.
-- src:4273 | that test remains applicable in his case until the end of the spell of incapacity beginning with that day or, as the case may be, in which that day falls, or until the 197th day of incapacity for work in that spell, whichever is the earlier.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4273)
GIVEN `the days of incapacity for work` IS A LIST OF DATE
      spell IS A Period
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 171B(3) the 197th day of incapacity for work in the spell` MEANS
    CONSIDER nth197
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): fewer than 197 days of incapacity lie in the spell, so there is no 197th day
    WHEN JUST serial THEN JUST (Date serial)
    WHERE
        serials MEANS map (GIVEN x YIELD Day x) `the days of incapacity for work`
        inSpell MEANS sort (nub (filter (GIVEN s YIELD ((Day (spell's `the first day`)) AT MOST s) AND (s AT MOST (Day (spell's `the last day`)))) serials))
        nth197 MEANS `s 171B(3) the nth day of incapacity for work` 197 inSpell

-- (3) the state of the test on a day that lies in the spell: the end of the spell, or the 197th day of incapacity in it, whichever is earlier.
-- src:4273 | that test remains applicable in his case until the end of the spell of incapacity beginning with that day or, as the case may be, in which that day falls, or until the 197th day of incapacity for work in that spell, whichever is the earlier.
-- src:4274 | For this purpose a “spell of incapacity” means a series of 4 or more consecutive days of incapacity for work; and any two such spells not separated by a period of more than 8 weeks shall be treated as one spell of incapacity.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4273-4274)
GIVEN `the days of incapacity for work` IS A LIST OF DATE
      spell IS A Period
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A `s 171B(3) status of the own occupation test`
`s 171B(3) the status of the own occupation test within the spell` MEANS
    IF day GREATER THAN spell's `the last day`
    THEN `s 171B(3) the stated period has ended`
    ELSE CONSIDER day197
         WHEN NOTHING THEN `s 171B(3) the test remains applicable`
         WHEN JUST d197 THEN
             IF day LESS THAN d197
             THEN `s 171B(3) the test remains applicable`
             ELSE (IF day EQUALS d197
                   THEN `s 171B(3) the text does not say whether the test applies`   -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "until the 197th day" does not say whether the 197th day itself is inside
                   ELSE `s 171B(3) the stated period has ended`)
    WHERE
        day197 MEANS `s 171B(3) the 197th day of incapacity for work in the spell` `the days of incapacity for work` spell

-- (3) "Where ... it is determined in relation to a person (a) that the test applicable with respect to any day is the own occupation
-- test, and (b) that he is on that test incapable of work, that test remains applicable in his case until the end of the spell of
-- incapacity beginning with that day or, as the case may be, in which that day falls, or until the 197th day of incapacity for work
-- in that spell, whichever is the earlier."  Day 1 is the first day of the determination.  Two fork points are in NOTES: whether the
-- 197th day is itself inside "until" (the text does not say: not covered), and whether days of incapacity that lie inside a merged
-- spell but outside any series of 4 or more count towards the 197 (taken as yes).
-- Regulations under s 171B(8) may change the 4 days and the 8 weeks: if they do, the text alone cannot answer.
-- src:4270 | (3) Where for any purpose of this Act it is determined in relation to a person—
-- src:4271 | (a) that the test applicable with respect to any day is the own occupation test, and
-- src:4272 | (b) that he is on that test incapable of work,
-- src:4273 | that test remains applicable in his case until the end of the spell of incapacity beginning with that day or, as the case may be, in which that day falls, or until the 197th day of incapacity for work in that spell, whichever is the earlier.
-- src:4274 | For this purpose a “spell of incapacity” means a series of 4 or more consecutive days of incapacity for work; and any two such spells not separated by a period of more than 8 weeks shall be treated as one spell of incapacity.
@ref s 171B(3) (src:4270-4274)
GIVEN `the days of incapacity for work` IS A LIST OF DATE
      determined IS A DATE
      day IS A DATE
      `regulations under subsection (8) change the figures in subsection (3)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `s 171B(3) status of the own occupation test`
`s 171B(3) the status of the own occupation test on the day` MEANS
    IF `regulations under subsection (8) change the figures in subsection (3)`
    THEN `s 171B(3) the text does not say whether the test applies`   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): subsection (8), "The Secretary of State may by regulations provide that subsection (3) above shall have effect as if" other figures
    ELSE
        IF day LESS THAN determined
        THEN `s 171B(3) there is no determination to carry forward`
        ELSE
            CONSIDER theSpell
            WHEN NOTHING THEN `s 171B(3) the text does not say whether the test applies`   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the determination day is not in a series of 4 or more consecutive days, so there is no spell for the test to last through
            WHEN JUST spell THEN `s 171B(3) the status of the own occupation test within the spell` `the days of incapacity for work` spell day
    WHERE
        theSpell MEANS `s 171B(3) the spell in which the day falls` `the days of incapacity for work` determined

-- (4) "For the purposes of subsection (3) above a day of incapacity for work means a day (a) with respect to which it has been
-- determined for any purpose of this Act that the person in question was incapable of work, or (b) in respect of which he was
-- entitled to statutory sick pay, or (c) in the case of a woman, which falls within the maternity allowance period, or (d) which in
-- accordance with regulations is to be treated for those purposes as a day of incapacity for work."  "Means" closes the list.
-- src:4275 | (4) For the purposes of subsection (3) above a day of incapacity for work means a day—
-- src:4276 | (a) with respect to which it has been determined for any purpose of this Act that the person in question was incapable of work, or
-- src:4277 | (b) in respect of which he was entitled to statutory sick pay, or
-- src:4278 | (c) in the case of a woman, which falls within the maternity allowance period, or
-- src:4279 | (d) which in accordance with regulations is to be treated for those purposes as a day of incapacity for work.
@ref s 171B(4) (src:4275-4279)
GIVEN person IS A Person
      `it has been determined for a purpose of this Act that the person was incapable of work on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person was entitled to statutory sick pay in respect of the day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day falls within the maternity allowance period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in accordance with regulations the day is to be treated as a day of incapacity for work` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171B(4) the day is a day of incapacity for work` MEANS
    IF     `it has been determined for a purpose of this Act that the person was incapable of work on the day`
        OR `the person was entitled to statutory sick pay in respect of the day`
        OR (person's `sex` EQUALS Woman AND `the day falls within the maternity allowance period`)
        OR `in accordance with regulations the day is to be treated as a day of incapacity for work`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a day of incapacity for work means a day" determined incapable, or of statutory sick pay, or (for a woman) within the maternity allowance period, or treated as such under regulations

-- (5) "Any provision of this Act apart from subsection (4) above under or by virtue of which a day is or is not to be treated for any
-- purpose as a day of incapacity for work shall be disregarded for the purposes of this section."
-- src:4280 | (5) Any provision of this Act apart from subsection (4) above under or by virtue of which a day is or is not to be treated for any purpose as a day of incapacity for work shall be disregarded for the purposes of this section.
@ref s 171B(5) (src:4280)
GIVEN `the provision is a provision of this Act under or by virtue of which a day is or is not treated as a day of incapacity for work` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provision is subsection (4) of this section` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171B(5) the provision is disregarded for the purposes of section 171B` MEANS
    IF NOT `the provision is a provision of this Act under or by virtue of which a day is or is not treated as a day of incapacity for work`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks only of provisions that treat a day as, or not as, a day of incapacity for work
    ELSE IF `the provision is subsection (4) of this section`
         THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Any provision of this Act apart from subsection (4) above"
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- (6) to (8): the power to define "remunerative work", the power to apply the section to a person with more than one occupation,
-- and the power to change the figures in subsection (3): inert, machinery.  Their effect on subsection (3) is the gate input above.

§§ `s 171C Personal capability assessments`

-- Nouns.
DECLARE `s 171C(1) the test that decides whether the person is capable or incapable of work` IS ONE OF
    `s 171C(1) the own occupation test`
    `s 171C(1) a personal capability assessment`
    `s 171C(1) the text does not say which test decides`

-- (1) "Where the own occupation test is not applicable, or has ceased to apply, in the case of a person, the question whether the
-- person is capable or incapable of work shall be determined in accordance with a personal capability assessment."
-- "Not applicable" is read as: s 171B(1) does not make it applicable and s 171B(3) does not carry it forward (fork in NOTES).
-- src:4290 | (1) Where the own occupation test is not applicable, or has ceased to apply, in the case of a person, the question whether the person is capable or incapable of work shall be determined in accordance with a personal capability assessment.
@ref s 171C(1) (src:4290)
GIVEN `s 171B(1) the own occupation test is applicable` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 171B(3) the status of the own occupation test on the day` IS A `s 171B(3) status of the own occupation test`
GIVETH A `s 171C(1) the test that decides whether the person is capable or incapable of work`
`s 171C(1) the test that decides` MEANS
    IF `s 171B(1) the own occupation test is applicable` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN `s 171C(1) the own occupation test`
    ELSE CONSIDER `s 171B(3) the status of the own occupation test on the day`
         WHEN `s 171B(3) the test remains applicable` THEN `s 171C(1) the own occupation test`
         WHEN `s 171B(3) the stated period has ended` THEN `s 171C(1) a personal capability assessment`
         WHEN `s 171B(3) there is no determination to carry forward` THEN `s 171C(1) a personal capability assessment`
         WHEN `s 171B(3) the text does not say whether the test applies` THEN `s 171C(1) the text does not say which test decides`

-- (2) "Provision shall be made by regulations (a) defining a personal capability assessment ...; (b) as to the manner of assessing":
-- an instruction to the maker of regulations (inert, machinery).  The outcome of an assessment is a plain input below.

-- (3) "Regulations may provide that, in any prescribed circumstances, a person to whom subsection (1) above applies shall, if the
-- prescribed conditions are met, be treated as incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment until such time
-- as (a) such an assessment has been carried out in his case, or (b) he falls to be treated as capable of work in accordance with
-- regulations under section 171A(2) or (3) above or section 171E below."
-- src:4294 | (3) Regulations may provide that, in any prescribed circumstances, a person to whom subsection (1) above applies shall, if the prescribed conditions are met, be treated as incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment until such time as—
-- src:4295 | (a) such an assessment has been carried out in his case, or
-- src:4296 | (b) he falls to be treated as capable of work in accordance with regulations under section 171A(2) or (3) above or section 171E below.
@ref s 171C(3) (src:4294-4296)
GIVEN `regulations under subsection (3) so provide` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is in the prescribed circumstances and the prescribed conditions are met` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a personal capability assessment has been carried out in the person's case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person falls to be treated as capable of work under regulations under section 171A(2) or (3) or section 171E` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171C(3) the person is treated as incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment` MEANS
    IF NOT `regulations under subsection (3) so provide`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may provide that"
    ELSE IF     `the person is in the prescribed circumstances and the prescribed conditions are met`
            AND (NOT `a personal capability assessment has been carried out in the person's case`)
            AND (NOT `the person falls to be treated as capable of work under regulations under section 171A(2) or (3) or section 171E`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the treatment lasts "until" an assessment has been carried out or the person falls to be treated as capable, and the subsection says nothing for a person outside the prescribed circumstances or conditions

-- (3), the last sentence: "The prescribed conditions may include the condition that it has not previously been determined, within such period as
-- may be prescribed, that the person in question is or is to be treated as capable of work."  A statement of what regulations may contain:
-- inert, machinery.

-- (4) "Except in prescribed circumstances, a personal capability assessment carried out in the case of a person before the time when
-- subsection (1) above applies to him shall be as effective for the purposes of that subsection as one carried out thereafter."
-- src:4298 | (4) Except in prescribed circumstances, a personal capability assessment carried out in the case of a person before the time when subsection (1) above applies to him shall be as effective for the purposes of that subsection as one carried out thereafter.
@ref s 171C(4) (src:4298)
GIVEN `the case is one of the prescribed circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171C(4) the earlier assessment is as effective as a later one` MEANS
    IF `the case is one of the prescribed circumstances`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Except in prescribed circumstances"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- (5) "The Secretary of State may, in the case of a person who for any purpose of this Act has been determined to be incapable of
-- work in accordance with a personal capability assessment (including one carried out by virtue of this subsection), require the
-- question whether the person is capable or incapable of work to be determined afresh in accordance with a further personal capability assessment."
-- src:4299 | (5) The Secretary of State may, in the case of a person who for any purpose of this Act has been determined to be incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment (including one carried out by virtue of this subsection), require the question whether the person is capable or incapable of work to be determined afresh in accordance with a further personal capability assessment.
@ref s 171C(5) (src:4299)
GIVEN `the person has for a purpose of this Act been determined to be incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171C(5) the Secretary of State may require the question to be determined afresh` MEANS
    IF `the person has for a purpose of this Act been determined to be incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power is "in the case of a person who ... has been determined to be incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment"

§§ `s 171D Incapacity for work: persons to be treated as incapable or capable of work`

-- Nouns: what regulations under subsection (1) do to the person in the case.
DECLARE `s 171D(1) treatment of the person` IS ONE OF
    `s 171D(1) treated as capable of work`
    `s 171D(1) treated as incapable of work`
    `s 171D(1) no treatment under this subsection applies`
    `s 171D(1) regulations give both treatments`

-- (1) "Regulations may provide that a person shall be treated as capable of work, or as incapable of work, in such cases or
-- circumstances as may be prescribed."
-- src:4301 | (1) Regulations may provide that a person shall be treated as capable of work, or as incapable of work, in such cases or circumstances as may be prescribed.
@ref s 171D(1) (src:4301)
GIVEN `regulations treat the person as capable of work in the case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations treat the person as incapable of work in the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `s 171D(1) treatment of the person`
`s 171D(1) the treatment of the person` MEANS
    IF `regulations treat the person as capable of work in the case` AND `regulations treat the person as incapable of work in the case`
    THEN `s 171D(1) regulations give both treatments`   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not say which treatment prevails when regulations give both
    ELSE IF `regulations treat the person as capable of work in the case`
         THEN `s 171D(1) treated as capable of work`
         ELSE IF `regulations treat the person as incapable of work in the case`
              THEN `s 171D(1) treated as incapable of work`
              ELSE `s 171D(1) no treatment under this subsection applies`   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may provide"; none applies

-- (2) Regulations may in particular treat a person as capable of work who does work of a prescribed description, and may provide
-- that a person is not so treated by reason only of doing prescribed work: powers (inert, machinery).

§§ `s 171E Incapacity for work: disqualification, &c.`

-- (1) "Regulations may provide for disqualifying a person for receiving any benefit, allowance or other advantage ..., or, in such
-- cases as may be prescribed, provide that a person shall be treated as capable of work, if (a) he has become incapable of work
-- through his own misconduct; (b) he fails without good cause to attend for or submit himself to such medical or other treatment as
-- may be required in accordance with the regulations; or (c) he fails without good cause to observe any prescribed rules of behaviour."
-- Two limbs, one function each, over the same three conditions.  The failures "without good cause" are stated as the person's
-- failure with no good cause.
-- src:4305 | (1) Regulations may provide for disqualifying a person for receiving any benefit, allowance or other advantage under any provision for the purposes of which this Part of this Act applies, or, in such cases as may be prescribed, provide that a person shall be treated as capable of work, if—
-- src:4306 | (a) he has become incapable of work through his own misconduct;
-- src:4307 | (b) he fails without good cause to attend for or submit himself to such medical or other treatment as may be required in accordance with the regulations; or
-- src:4308 | (c) he fails without good cause to observe any prescribed rules of behaviour.
@ref s 171E(1) (src:4305-4308)
GIVEN `regulations under subsection (1) provide for disqualifying a person in the case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has become incapable of work through the person's own misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person fails without good cause to attend for or submit to the required medical or other treatment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person fails without good cause to observe the prescribed rules of behaviour` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171E(1) the person is disqualified for receiving a benefit, allowance or other advantage` MEANS
    IF NOT `regulations under subsection (1) provide for disqualifying a person in the case`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may provide for disqualifying"
    ELSE IF     `the person has become incapable of work through the person's own misconduct`
             OR `the person fails without good cause to attend for or submit to the required medical or other treatment`
             OR `the person fails without good cause to observe the prescribed rules of behaviour`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if—" ... "he has become incapable of work through his own misconduct" ... "he fails without good cause to attend for or submit himself to such medical or other treatment" ... "he fails without good cause to observe any prescribed rules of behaviour" (a failed "if"; reading (a))

-- src:4305 | (1) Regulations may provide for disqualifying a person for receiving any benefit, allowance or other advantage under any provision for the purposes of which this Part of this Act applies, or, in such cases as may be prescribed, provide that a person shall be treated as capable of work, if—
-- src:4306 | (a) he has become incapable of work through his own misconduct;
-- src:4307 | (b) he fails without good cause to attend for or submit himself to such medical or other treatment as may be required in accordance with the regulations; or
-- src:4308 | (c) he fails without good cause to observe any prescribed rules of behaviour.
@ref s 171E(1) (src:4305-4308)
GIVEN `regulations under subsection (1) provide that a person is treated as capable of work in the case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has become incapable of work through the person's own misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person fails without good cause to attend for or submit to the required medical or other treatment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person fails without good cause to observe the prescribed rules of behaviour` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171E(1) the person is treated as capable of work` MEANS
    IF NOT `regulations under subsection (1) provide that a person is treated as capable of work in the case`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "in such cases as may be prescribed, provide that a person shall be treated as capable of work"
    ELSE IF     `the person has become incapable of work through the person's own misconduct`
             OR `the person fails without good cause to attend for or submit to the required medical or other treatment`
             OR `the person fails without good cause to observe the prescribed rules of behaviour`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if—" ... "he has become incapable of work through his own misconduct" ... "he fails without good cause to attend for or submit himself to such medical or other treatment" ... "he fails without good cause to observe any prescribed rules of behaviour" (a failed "if"; reading (a))

-- (2) "Regulations shall provide that any such disqualification shall be, or as the case may be that the person shall be treated as
-- capable of work, for such period not exceeding 6 weeks as may be determined in accordance with Chapter II of Part I of the Social
-- Security Act 1998."  Six weeks are 42 days (s 171G(2)); the period is given in days.
-- src:4309 | (2) Regulations shall provide that any such disqualification shall be, or as the case may be that the person shall be treated as capable of work, for such period not exceeding 6 weeks as may be determined in accordance with Chapter II of Part I of the Social Security Act 1998.
@ref s 171E(2) (src:4309)
GIVEN `the period of the disqualification or treatment as capable of work in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171E(2) the period of the disqualification or treatment as capable of work is allowed` MEANS
    IF `the period of the disqualification or treatment as capable of work in days` AT MOST 42
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "for such period not exceeding 6 weeks"

-- (3) Regulations may prescribe the matters that bear on good cause: a power (inert, machinery).

§§ `s 171F Incapacity for work: work as councillor to be disregarded`

-- (1) "In determining whether a person is capable or incapable of work, there shall be disregarded any work which that person has undertaken as a councillor."
-- src:4314 | (1) In determining whether a person is capable or incapable of work, there shall be disregarded any work which that person has undertaken as a councillor.
@ref s 171F(1) (src:4314)
GIVEN `the work was undertaken as a councillor` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171F(1) the work is disregarded in determining whether the person is capable or incapable of work` MEANS
    IF `the work was undertaken as a councillor`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection disregards "work which that person has undertaken as a councillor" and says nothing of other work

-- (2) Nouns: the part of Great Britain and the councils the definition names.
DECLARE `s 171F(2) part of Great Britain` IS ONE OF
    `s 171F(2) England and Wales`
    `s 171F(2) Scotland`

DECLARE `s 171F(2) kind of council` IS ONE OF
    `s 171F(2)(a) a London borough council`
    `s 171F(2)(a) a county or county borough council`
    `s 171F(2)(a) a district council`
    `s 171F(2)(a) a parish or community council`
    `s 171F(2)(a) the Common Council of the City of London`
    `s 171F(2)(a) the Council of the Isles of Scilly`
    `s 171F(2)(b) a regional council`
    `s 171F(2)(b) an islands council`
    `s 171F(2)(b) a district council`
    `s 171F(2)(c) another kind of council`

-- (2) "For this purpose 'councillor' means (a) in relation to England and Wales, a member of a London borough council, a county or
-- county borough council, a district council, a parish or community council, the Common Council of the City of London or the Council
-- of the Isles of Scilly; and (b) in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council."  "Means" closes the list.
-- src:4315 | (2) For this purpose “councillor” means—
-- src:4316 | (a) in relation to England and Wales, a member of a London borough council, a county or county borough council, a district council, a parish or community council, the Common Council of the City of London or the Council of the Isles of Scilly; and
-- src:4317 | (b) in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council.
@ref s 171F(2) (src:4315-4317)
GIVEN place IS A `s 171F(2) part of Great Britain`
      council IS A `s 171F(2) kind of council`
      `the person is a member of the council` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171F(2) the person is a councillor` MEANS
    IF NOT `the person is a member of the council`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“councillor” means—" ... "a member of" one of the listed councils
    ELSE CONSIDER place
         WHEN `s 171F(2) England and Wales` THEN
             CONSIDER council
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a London borough council` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a county or county borough council` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a district council` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a parish or community council` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) the Common Council of the City of London` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) the Council of the Isles of Scilly` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(b) a regional council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to England and Wales, a member of" the listed councils, which do not include a regional council
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(b) an islands council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to England and Wales, a member of" the listed councils, which do not include an islands council
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(b) a district council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to England and Wales, a member of" the listed councils; the district council of limb (b) is a Scottish one
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(c) another kind of council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“councillor” means" a member of one of the listed councils
         WHEN `s 171F(2) Scotland` THEN
             CONSIDER council
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(b) a regional council` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(b) an islands council` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(b) a district council` THEN JUST TRUE
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a London borough council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council"
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a county or county borough council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council"
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a district council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council"; the district council of limb (a) is an English or Welsh one
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) a parish or community council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council"
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) the Common Council of the City of London` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council"
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(a) the Council of the Isles of Scilly` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council"
             WHEN `s 171F(2)(c) another kind of council` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to Scotland, a member of a regional, islands or district council"

-- (3) "The reference in subsection (1) above to the work which a person undertakes as a councillor shall be taken to include any work
-- which he undertakes as a member of any of the bodies referred to in section 177(1) of the Local Government Act 1972, or section 49(1)
-- or (1A) of the Local Government (Scotland) Act 1973, of which he is a member by virtue of his being a councillor."
-- src:4318 | (3) The reference in subsection (1) above to the work which a person undertakes as a councillor shall be taken to include any work which he undertakes as a member of any of the bodies referred to in section 177(1) of the Local Government Act 1972, or section 49(1) or (1A) of the Local Government (Scotland) Act 1973, of which he is a member by virtue of his being a councillor.
@ref s 171F(3) (src:4318)
GIVEN `the work was undertaken as a member of a body referred to in section 177(1) of the Local Government Act 1972 or section 49(1) or (1A) of the Local Government (Scotland) Act 1973` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a member of the body by virtue of being a councillor` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171F(3) the work counts as work undertaken as a councillor` MEANS
    IF `the work was undertaken as a member of a body referred to in section 177(1) of the Local Government Act 1972 or section 49(1) or (1A) of the Local Government (Scotland) Act 1973`
       AND `the person is a member of the body by virtue of being a councillor`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "shall be taken to include" adds a case and does not close what counts as work undertaken as a councillor

-- (4) "The 'pre-commencement period' means the period beginning with 11th May 1987 and ending immediately before 9th October 1989."
-- src:4322 | The “pre-commencement period” means the period beginning with 11th May 1987 and ending immediately before 9th October 1989 (the coming into force of paragraph 2 of Schedule 8 to the Social Security Act 1989 which made provision corresponding to the provision made by this section).
@ref s 171F(4) (src:4322)
`s 171F(4) the pre-commencement period` MEANS Period WITH
    `the first day` IS Date 11 5 1987
    `the last day`  IS Date 8 10 1989

-- (4) "In making any such determination as is mentioned in subsection (1) above a person shall be treated as having been incapable
-- of work on any day which falls in the pre-commencement period and which (a) would have been treated as a day on which he was so
-- incapable, were there disregarded any work which he undertook (or was capable of undertaking) as a councillor; but (b) would not
-- have been so treated apart from this subsection."
-- src:4319 | (4) In making any such determination as is mentioned in subsection (1) above a person shall be treated as having been incapable of work on any day which falls in the pre-commencement period and which—
-- src:4320 | (a) would have been treated as a day on which he was so incapable, were there disregarded any work which he undertook (or was capable of undertaking) as a councillor; but
-- src:4321 | (b) would not have been so treated apart from this subsection.
@ref s 171F(4) (src:4319-4321)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `the day would have been treated as a day of incapacity were work as a councillor disregarded` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day would not have been so treated apart from this subsection` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171F(4) the person is treated as having been incapable of work on the day` MEANS
    IF     (Day day) AT LEAST (Day (`s 171F(4) the pre-commencement period`'s `the first day`))
       AND (Day day) AT MOST (Day (`s 171F(4) the pre-commencement period`'s `the last day`))
       AND `the day would have been treated as a day of incapacity were work as a councillor disregarded`
       AND `the day would not have been so treated apart from this subsection`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the treatment is for "any day which falls in the pre-commencement period" meeting both (a) and (b)

§§ `s 171G Incapacity for work: supplementary provisions`

-- (1) "The provisions of this Part of this Act do not apply (a) for the purposes of Part V of this Act (benefit for industrial
-- injuries: see section 94(6) above); (b) for the purposes of Part XI of this Act (statutory sick pay: see section 151(4) above); or
-- (c) for such other purposes as may be prescribed."
-- src:4324 | (1) The provisions of this Part of this Act do not apply—
-- src:4325 | (a) for the purposes of Part V of this Act (benefit for industrial injuries: see section 94(6) above);
-- src:4326 | (b) for the purposes of Part XI of this Act (statutory sick pay: see section 151(4) above); or
-- src:4327 | (c) for such other purposes as may be prescribed.
@ref s 171G(1) (src:4324-4327)
GIVEN `the purpose is that of Part V of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the purpose is that of Part XI of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the purpose is another purpose prescribed by regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171G(1) this Part applies for the purpose` MEANS
    IF     `the purpose is that of Part V of this Act`
        OR `the purpose is that of Part XI of this Act`
        OR `the purpose is another purpose prescribed by regulations`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The provisions of this Part of this Act do not apply"
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): any other purpose is for s 171A(1)

-- (2) "'prescribed' means specified in or determined in accordance with regulations".
-- src:4328 | (2) In this Part of this Act—
-- src:4329 | “prescribed” means specified in or determined in accordance with regulations; and
@ref s 171G(2) (src:4328-4329)
GIVEN `the matter is specified in or determined in accordance with regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171G(2) the matter is prescribed` MEANS
    IF `the matter is specified in or determined in accordance with regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“prescribed” means specified in or determined in accordance with regulations"

-- (2) "'week' means any period of 7 days": the core's `any period of 7 days`.  Both ends of the period are counted.
-- src:4330 | “week” means any period of 7 days.
@ref s 171G(2) (src:4330)
GIVEN period IS A Period
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 171G(2) the period is a week` MEANS
    (Day (period's `the last day`) MINUS Day (period's `the first day`)) PLUS 1 EQUALS 7

-- src:4330 | “week” means any period of 7 days.
@ref s 171G(2) (src:4330)
`s 171G(2) the week convention` MEANS `any period of 7 days`

§§ `s 171A(1) the person is incapable of work: the whole Part, applied to one day`

-- The seam S35.  Another Part asks "is the person incapable of work?" and this is the answer the Part gives: it
-- composes the functions above, each of which has already said what the text does and does not say.
-- Order, from the text: (1) the Part must apply to the purpose (s 171G(1)) and nothing else may expressly provide otherwise (s 171A(1));
-- regulations that treat the person as capable of work under ss 171A(2), 171A(3), 171E(1) or s 171D(1) decide the question;
-- regulations that treat the person as incapable (s 171D(1)) likewise; otherwise the test that s 171C(1) selects decides: the
-- own occupation test (s 171B(2)) or a personal capability assessment (outcome supplied by the caller: regulations define it).
-- The text does not say which treatment prevails when regulations give opposite treatments: that case is not covered.
-- src:4254 | (1) For the purposes of this Act, save as otherwise expressly provided, whether a person is capable or incapable of work shall be determined in accordance with the provisions of this Part of this Act.
@ref s 171A(1) (src:4254)
GIVEN `s 171G(1) this Part applies for the purpose` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 171A(1) this Part determines whether the person is capable or incapable of work` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 171A(2) the person is treated as capable of work` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 171A(3) the person is treated as capable of work` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 171E(1) the person is treated as capable of work` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 171D(1) the treatment of the person` IS A `s 171D(1) treatment of the person`
      `s 171C(1) the test that decides` IS A `s 171C(1) the test that decides whether the person is capable or incapable of work`
      `s 171B(2) the person is incapable of work on the own occupation test` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person is incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171A(1) the person is incapable of work` MEANS
    IF `s 171G(1) this Part applies for the purpose` EQUALS JUST FALSE
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): s 171G(1), "The provisions of this Part of this Act do not apply" for the purpose; the provision that applies instead answers
    ELSE
        IF NOT (`s 171A(1) this Part determines whether the person is capable or incapable of work` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 171A(1), "save as otherwise expressly provided"
        ELSE
            CONSIDER `s 171D(1) the treatment of the person`
            WHEN `s 171D(1) regulations give both treatments` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): regulations treat the person as capable of work and as incapable of work, and the text does not say which prevails
            WHEN `s 171D(1) treated as capable of work` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person shall be treated as capable of work" (s 171D(1))
            WHEN `s 171D(1) treated as incapable of work` THEN
                IF capableByRegulations
                THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): regulations under s 171D(1) treat the person as incapable of work and regulations under s 171A(2), 171A(3) or 171E(1) as capable of work, and the text does not say which prevails
                ELSE JUST TRUE
            WHEN `s 171D(1) no treatment under this subsection applies` THEN
                IF capableByRegulations
                THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "he shall be treated as capable of work" (ss 171A(2), 171A(3), 171E(1))
                ELSE decided
    WHERE
        capableByRegulations MEANS
            (`s 171A(2) the person is treated as capable of work` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
            OR (`s 171A(3) the person is treated as capable of work` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
            OR (`s 171E(1) the person is treated as capable of work` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        decided MEANS
            CONSIDER `s 171C(1) the test that decides`
            WHEN `s 171C(1) the own occupation test` THEN `s 171B(2) the person is incapable of work on the own occupation test`
            WHEN `s 171C(1) a personal capability assessment` THEN `the person is incapable of work in accordance with a personal capability assessment`
            WHEN `s 171C(1) the text does not say which test decides` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): s 171B(3), "until the 197th day of incapacity for work", does not say whether the 197th day is inside
